From ac9ee5b99c17dd795e9e02275337ac10f60d15b2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Viet Hung Nguyen <hvn@calif.io>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 16:26:46 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] feat: Optionally decode an Avro uuid as an ordinary string

Avro defines `uuid` as an annotation on `string`, and a reader is free to leave
the annotation uninterpreted. avro-cpp does exactly that: it never parses or
validates a uuid, so whatever text was written comes back unchanged. Java's
behaviour is the same for a `Utf8`-backed field.

Parsing it into a `Uuid` has three consequences a reader that treats it as a
string does not have, and each of them shows up as a difference against those
implementations:

- The bytes change. `Uuid` re-encodes in canonical hyphenated lowercase, so
  `urn:uuid:` prefixes, braces, upper-case hex and unhyphenated 32-hex forms are
  all rewritten. A value read and written back is not the value that arrived,
  which anyone comparing bytes or checking a signature over them will see.
- A 16-byte string is reinterpreted. Any uuid field holding exactly 16 bytes is
  taken as a raw `Uuid`, so an ordinary 16-character string comes back as 36
  characters of hex. Length decides how the field is read, which also means the
  UTF-8 check below is skipped at exactly that length.
- Malformed text is rejected, making data such implementations wrote unreadable.

`util::set_uuid_as_string` opts out of all three: with it on, `Schema::Uuid`
decodes exactly as `Schema::String`. It is off by default, so nothing changes
for an existing consumer until a caller asks for it, once per process and before
any decoding.

It composes with `set_non_utf8_string_as_bytes` rather than duplicating it: a
uuid whose text is not valid UTF-8 yields `Value::Bytes` when that setting is
also on, and still fails when it is not. The two are independent, which
`non_utf8_still_fails_without_the_other_setting` pins.

`Value::String` was already accepted at a `Schema::Uuid` position by `validate`
and by the encoder, so the decoded value stays usable and round-trips byte for
byte. What needed changing beyond decode is `resolve_uuid`, which would
otherwise parse the string straight back into a `Uuid` and reintroduce the
canonicalisation, plus the `Value::Bytes` arms of `validate` and `encode` for
the non-UTF-8 case.

Tests follow the split the other set-once settings use: the parsing behaviour
stays covered by the existing unit tests at the default, and the accepting cases
live in their own binary, `avro/tests/uuid_as_string.rs`, because a `OnceLock`
cannot be reset within a process.

No public signature changes and no new error variants.
